      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Is Hessonite always brown or orange?</strong>
        <p>No. Natural Hessonite ranges from honey and amber through cinnamon to cognac. Very dark or muddy stones are typically lower quality. The finest stones have a warm, earthy glow that feels alive rather than flat or dull.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Is Hessonite usually treated?</strong>
        <p>Most quality Hessonite is untreated. Light heat treatment may occur and must always be disclosed. We carry only stones with full treatment transparency and independent certification.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Can it be worn daily?</strong>
        <p>Yes. Hessonite is durable enough for regular daily wear when properly set and cared for with basic mindfulness.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Can Hessonite be worn with other gemstones?</strong>
        <p>Yes — but combinations should always be guided by the individual chart. Do not combine gemstones without proper astrological assessment.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>How soon do effects show?</strong>
        <p>Typically within 30–60 days, depending on Rahu's placement and the specifics of the individual horoscope.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Which metal should it be set in?</strong>
        <p>Silver is traditionally preferred. Gold may be used when astrologically advised.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'cats-eye' -%}
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>What exactly is Cat's Eye?</strong>
        <p>A gemstone exhibiting chatoyancy — a moving band of reflected light caused by parallel needle-like inclusions within the stone. In Vedic astrology, Chrysoberyl Cat's Eye is the classical choice for ☋ Ketu.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Are all Cat's Eye stones astrologically effective?</strong>
        <p>No. Only Chrysoberyl Cat's Eye is the true Lehsunia. Quartz Cat's Eye has limited, chart-dependent use. Fibre-optic, glass, and synthetic versions have no astrological value regardless of how convincing the eye appears.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>What makes a good Cat's Eye?</strong>
        <p>A sharp, straight, well-centred eye that runs continuously from end to end and is clearly visible in normal light. Strong contrast between the bright eye and the body colour is essential. A broken, diffused, or double line indicates poor quality.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Is Cat's Eye risky to wear without consultation?</strong>
        <p>Yes. Ketu is among the most powerful planetary influences. Cat's Eye should only be worn after expert astrological consultation and proper chart analysis — never casually or based on general recommendations.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Can it be worn daily?</strong>
        <p>Yes, when properly set. Chrysoberyl is durable at Mohs 8.5 and well suited to regular wear with basic care.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Which metal is best?</strong>
        <p>Silver is traditionally preferred. Gold may be used when astrologically advised.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'amethyst' -%}
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Is Amethyst a natural gemstone?</strong>
        <p>Yes. It is a natural variety of quartz, formed in the earth and not synthetic or composite. Most Amethyst is untreated — confirm this through independent certification before purchasing.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Does Amethyst fade over time?</strong>
        <p>High-quality natural Amethyst is colour-stable under ordinary wear. Prolonged exposure to heat or strong direct sunlight may gradually lighten the colour — normal daily wear does not present this risk.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Is darker always better?</strong>
        <p>No. The best stones show balanced, even colour with good clarity — rich without being so dark that the stone loses transparency and life. Gentle natural colour zoning is acceptable; harsh or uneven banding lowers quality.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Can it be worn daily?</strong>
        <p>Yes. At Mohs 7, Amethyst is suitable for daily wear when properly set. A protective setting is advisable for rings worn during physical activity.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Is Amethyst used in meditation or spiritual practice?</strong>
        <p>Yes — it is widely associated with calm, focus, emotional balance, and meditative clarity, and has been used in contemplative traditions across cultures for centuries.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Which metal pairs best?</strong>
        <p>Silver and white metals are commonly preferred and complement the stone's violet tones naturally. Gold may also be used when preferred or astrologically advised.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'citrine' -%}
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Is Citrine a natural gemstone?</strong>
        <p>Yes. Citrine is a natural variety of quartz, formed in the earth. Most Amethyst in the market has been heat-treated to produce Citrine colour — always ask for clear disclosure and confirm the stone is natural Citrine, not converted Amethyst.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Is Citrine suitable for astrological use?</strong>
        <p>Yes. Associated with ♃ Guru (Jupiter), it is widely chosen as a gentler, more accessible alternative to Yellow Sapphire for Jupiter-related remedies.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Does Citrine fade or change colour?</strong>
        <p>Natural Citrine is colour-stable under ordinary wear. Prolonged exposure to heat or strong direct sunlight may gradually lighten it — normal daily use does not present this risk.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>What weight is recommended?</strong>
        <p>Citrine is commonly worn between 2–15 carats depending on personal preference and astrological advice. Quality and colour presence matter more than reaching a specific weight.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Can it be worn daily?</strong>
        <p>Yes. At Mohs 7, Citrine is suitable for daily wear with proper care. A protective setting is advisable for rings worn during physical activity.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Which metal pairs best?</strong>
        <p>Silver, Gold, and Panchdhatu are all commonly used — the choice may depend on design preference or astrological guidance.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'turquoise' -%}
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>What makes Turquoise different from other gems in this collection?</strong>
        <p>Turquoise is a porous, organic-feeling stone with a sensitivity to moisture, oils, and chemicals that crystalline gems do not share. This makes it more care-intensive, but also gives it a warmth and character that polished stones often lack.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Is stabilised Turquoise suitable for astrological use?</strong>
        <p>High-quality stabilised Turquoise is generally accepted when clearly disclosed. Natural, untreated stones are preferred where available and within budget. What is never acceptable is stabilisation or dyeing presented as natural.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Can Turquoise change colour over time?</strong>
        <p>Yes — if it absorbs sweat, oils, or chemicals it can darken permanently. This is a structural change, not a cleaning issue, and it is irreversible. Consistent care after every wear is the most effective prevention.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Which origin is better — Iranian or American?</strong>
        <p>Iranian Turquoise is the traditional benchmark for astrological use, valued for its fine texture and classic sky-blue colour. American Turquoise offers striking character and is well regarded for jewellery. For astrological purposes, Iranian origin is generally preferred when accessible.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Can Turquoise be worn daily?</strong>
        <p>With care and a protective setting, yes — though rotating wear and removing it before any chemical contact significantly extends its life and preserves its colour.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Which metal is best?</strong>
        <p>Silver is most commonly used. Gold when preferred or astrologically advised. A bezel or semi-bezel setting is strongly recommended over prong settings for this stone.</p>
      </div>
  {%- endcase -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- comment -%} Check if we have mapped content for this collection {%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign has_mapped_content = false -%}
{%- capture handles -%}emerald,blue-sapphire,opal,ruby,yellow-sapphire,white-sapphire,red-coral,pearl,hessonite,cats-eye,amethyst,citrine,turquoise{%- endcapture -%}
